" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106594 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106627 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106627 #edb/ref 1061 164419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106627 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5440fe87-c485-5fa7-ab27-be4de4746fa1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106627 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106628 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106628 #edb/ref 1061 164420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106628 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c37fcdd-4a4f-5fa4-b419-cdfb144ce00e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106628 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106629 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106629 #edb/ref 1061 164423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106629 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52480c3c-c289-5e97-b7aa-1a5401a73156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106629 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106630 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106630 #edb/ref 1061 164426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106630 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b5d08f57-ce6b-5494-a967-38442f05a2c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106630 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106631 #edb/ref 1061 164429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106631 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec111c1e-1c82-5a70-b2f1-5dfba6037c19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106631 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107043 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that longer sides in a triangle are opposite to larger angles, and vice versa. But what if two (or more) sides have the same length?\n\nAs you might have guessed, if two sides have the same length, then the opposite angles have the same measure. And vice-versa: if two angles have the same measure, then the opposite sides have the same length.\n\nSo, we obtain the following important properties of isosceles and equilateral triangles:\n\n- A triangle is **equilateral** if and only if all of its angles are of $60^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/ad/ad56532db83ae54eeabf04d5a182a850bed14d9d0a65518b8048c3c0c112c371.png)\n\n- A triangle is **isosceles** if and only if it has two congruent angles.\n\n![](images/2b/2b5d6779527eae10e108ffcdc80745f63f3f38e41474673990bf74c83d2e6a64.png)\n\n**Note:** The statement above encompasses two theorems:\n\n- The **isosceles triangle theorem**:\n\n*If a triangle is isosceles, then the base angles are congruent.*\n\n- The **converse isosceles triangle theorem**: \n\n*If two angles in a triangle have the same measure, then the triangle must be isosceles.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107043 #edb/ref 1065 2227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107043 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "87d68609-ccd1-5ebd-b129-2c6993e05b75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107043 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107061 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $O$ be a point on the plane. Let's consider all the points on the plane whose distance to $O$ is equal to $5\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/ba/ba0fc787b4469b0322fa46fa072d58ae163e24dea284f3e5507306e9b30dfbf3.png)\n\nThe collection of all such points defines a geometrical shape which is called a **circle**. \n\n- The point $O$ is called the **center** of the circle.\n\n- The distance from $O$ to any point on the circle is called the **radius**.\n\nIn our diagram above, the radius is $r={\\color{black}5\\,\\textrm{cm}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107061 #edb/ref 1065 2417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107061 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a53c8e3d-2c5a-558c-912f-f2e051ef7b41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107061 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107062 #edb/ref 1064 "There are three important line segments associated with a circle:\n\n- A **radius** is any line segment that connects a point on the circle with the center (left-hand diagram).\n\n- A **chord** is any line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle (middle diagram).\n\n- A **diameter** is any chord that passes through the center of the circle (right-hand diagram). The diameter is the longest chord and its measure is twice the radius of the circle:\n\n$$\nd = 2r.\n$$\n\n![](images/7f/7f12a9db3210054e64397e26c87fd57ce4e55ff5dc9a2ed0d3b0ce3a8f9c2e65.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107062 #edb/ref 1065 2418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107062 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bcbdf407-5fe9-57d7-9359-167f8fbf21b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107062 #edb/ref 1067 "Chords and Diameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107137 #edb/ref 1064 "The sum of the interior angles of *any* triangle equals $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/fc/fcc21c5e13ab095da35eeca128e3a82a075d649e205aa0e1683b6ab0e4a4cc4c.png)\n\nAs it turns out, there is a general rule for the sum of the interior angles that applies to all polygons:\n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nFor example, every triangle has ${\\color{blue}3}$ sides. Let's substitute $n={\\color{blue}3}$ into the formula to check that it works:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (3-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a triangle equals $180^\\circ,$ which we know is correct.\n\nLet's see some more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107137 #edb/ref 1065 6654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107137 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8edf1574-04d8-5aea-839f-9c03c16e6ab5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107137 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107138 #edb/ref 1064 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).\n$$\n\nEvery quadrilateral has $n={\\color{blue}4}$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral equals $360^\\circ.$ \n\n![](images/a0/a0d6cd7aa4b4e7aba6451b32087fa6cf86e0f63d2ff70e68328a5ac9d4eddcfc.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107138 #edb/ref 1065 6688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107138 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1de6f70b-9797-56cc-b325-b5375efaf06b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107138 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sum of the Interior Angles of a Quadrilateral" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107139 #edb/ref 1064 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).\n$$\n\nEvery pentagon has $n={\\color{blue}5}$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon equals $540^\\circ.$ \n\n![](images/8d/8d0478edf5e40b947b06b7b6b0b726999b11ce39dc8af5daaf414f2bd2292647.png)\n\nWe can use the same formula to deduce the sum of the interior angles of hexagons, heptagons, octagons, and so forth." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107139 #edb/ref 1065 6738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107139 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "573fd3ba-e480-5a75-ba71-1fd15e977637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107139 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sum of the Interior Angles of a Pentagon" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107170 #edb/ref 1064 "We've made use of the following theorem throughout this lesson. \n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nThis formula works because any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles using diagonals that meet at one fixed vertex of the polygon. \n\nLet's illustrate this for a few different polygons.\n\n| ![](images/20/20aae267ba75bb418e509d548b1519af47b4cf9464d7f8400369c8e77b013aae.png) | **Quadrilateral** \n\nNumber of vertices: $4$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $4 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}} = 360^\\circ$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/20/20551ad9f4661402aef9e501bf9d4c841e671b4277fae78f85d9e9ada9830592.png) | **Pentagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $5$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $5 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{3}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} = 540^\\circ$ |\n| ![](images/de/de8ec9be742a40384891de3a682a151e817f6c24d624e0ef505fb98ce6a1e60d.png) | **Hexagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $6$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $6 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{4}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{4}} = 720^\\circ$ |\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of each polygon is the same as the sum of the interior angles of the corresponding triangles. \n\nEach triangle has interior angles that sum to $180^\\circ,$ and any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles in the manner shown above. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles in an $n$-sided polygon is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107170 #edb/ref 1065 9114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107170 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "211863f5-73c2-57af-83e9-696d21971d5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107170 #edb/ref 1067 "Justification of the Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110627 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110627 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110627 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110627 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110627 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110627 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110627 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110627 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110627 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110627 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Supplementary Angles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110627 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110627 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "931f7270-ec55-5c42-8c1b-6c7988f6c921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110628 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110628 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110628 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Supplementary Angles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110628 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110628 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2ba5d861-c6bf-57df-aa00-7034aeb5e1ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110629 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110629 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110629 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110629 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Supplementary Angles From a Description" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110629 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110629 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2c7eec3a-50e3-5550-9e1e-def64ca9642a" #edb/ref 175